  "notebook": {
    "name": "Markov Graphs: Spectral Mixing and Convergence",
    "cells": [
      {
        "type": "markdown",
        "language": "markdown",
        "name": "intro",
        "code": "# Markov Graphs: Spectral Mixing and Convergence\n\n**English review draft.** This is a fictional routing experiment, not a fleet planner or a claim about real delivery traffic. It needs only SciREPL's Python kernel, NumPy and built-in plotting; no package installation, API key or downloaded data.\n\n**Level: Advanced mathematics.** The intended reading pace is first-year graduate study in applied probability or applied mathematics. The practical preparation is normally third-year undergraduate linear algebra (eigenvalues and orthogonality) and probability (conditioning, expectation and variance); some familiarity with Markov chains helps. These are readiness guidelines, not enrollment requirements.\n\n**Running is easier than understanding every derivation.** You can run the workbook unchanged with basic SciREPL skills. Reading and modifying the code is easier if you know Python functions, loops, NumPy indexing and matrix multiplication. Allow additional background-study time if these mathematical or programming ideas are new to you.\n\nWe start with **one delivery truck**, then follow **two labelled trucks, A and B**. Their joint positions give a two-dimensional heatmap, echoing the two-sticker example in **Markov Groups**. The rules differ: these trucks move one at a time, and two trucks cannot occupy the same loading bay. Cube moves carry both stickers together and obey cube geometry.\n\nRun named code cells in order, or use the cell menu's **Run all below** action. The default record is 10,000 steps, seed 314159. Helper cells are longer than the experiments; their docstrings explain the conventions. You can read the pictures first.\n\n**Three questions, not one:** Where is the system after exactly $t$ moves? How accurate is a histogram of visits collected over $n$ moves? How much of its error is expected because consecutive visits are correlated? A spatially speckled picture alone answers none of these reliably.\n\nThe first plots need only probabilities and matrices. The spectral explanations are optional deeper reading, but run their helper cells if you want the later plots. The final two-state example separates periodicity from convergence."
      },
      {
        "type": "markdown",
        "language": "markdown",
        "name": "routing_bridge",
        "code": "## 1 · Two neighbourhoods and one bottleneck\n\nStops **0–5** form the west cycle; **6–11** form the east cycle. Stop 0 connects to stop 6. Each stop is a single loading bay, not a whole depot. A truck can move only along an edge or wait at its current stop.\n\nEvery within-neighbourhood edge has weight 1. We compare bridge weights $w=0.15$ (weak) and $w=1$ (strong), using the **same clock** in both cases. An edge of weight $a$ gives move probability $a/6$ in either direction; the leftover probability is waiting. This deliberately balanced routing is **not** the usual random walk that chooses uniformly among neighbours. The bridge weight is an imposed crossing probability, not congestion calculated from road traffic.\n\nLet $A$ be the symmetric edge-weight matrix, $D$ the diagonal matrix of weighted degrees, and $L=D-A$ the graph Laplacian. Then\n\n$$P=I-\\frac{1}{6}L,\\qquad P[d,j]=\\Pr(X_{t+1}=d\\mid X_t=j).$$\n\nColumns are source stops; rows are destination stops. At stop 0, two local moves each have probability $1/6$, crossing the bridge has probability $w/6$, and waiting has probability $1-(2+w)/6$. Waiting is at least $1/2$ everywhere. Positive moves connect the graph; waiting prevents forced alternation. The stationary probability is $\\pi_j=1/12$, because $P$ is symmetric and stochastic.\n\nRun `route_graph` to build both models, then `draw_routes` for the picture. Labels are stop indices throughout; west/east names refer to neighbourhoods, not to truck A/B."
      },
      {
        "type": "code",
        "language": "python",
        "name": "route_graph",
        "code": "\"\"\"Cell 1: a fictional, deliberately balanced delivery-route graph.\n\nOnly NumPy and the standard library are used.  Stops are single loading bays,\nnot measurements of a real fleet.  Matrix entries use P[destination, source].\n\"\"\"\n\nimport numpy as np\nfrom collections import deque\n\n\ndef build_stop_graph(bridge_weight, clock_degree=3.0):\n    \"\"\"Return two six-stop cycles joined at stops 0 and 6.\n\n    P = I - L / (2 * clock_degree) is symmetric and column stochastic.\n    Keeping clock_degree=3 for BOTH bridge weights gives one common clock:\n    strengthening the bridge does not rescale all the within-cycle moves.\n    Its diagonal is at least 1/2, so this lazy reversible kernel is PSD.\n    \"\"\"\n    bridge_weight = float(bridge_weight)\n    clock_degree = float(clock_degree)\n    if not np.isfinite(bridge_weight) or bridge_weight <= 0:\n        raise ValueError(\"The connecting bridge must have positive finite weight.\")\n    if not np.isfinite(clock_degree) or clock_degree <= 0:\n        raise ValueError(\"clock_degree must be positive and finite.\")\n\n    weights = np.zeros((12, 12), dtype=float)\n    edges = []\n    for offset in (0, 6):\n        for local_stop in range(6):\n            first = offset + local_stop\n            second = offset + (local_stop + 1) % 6\n            weights[first, second] = weights[second, first] = 1.0\n            edges.append((first, second, 1.0))\n    weights[0, 6] = weights[6, 0] = bridge_weight\n    edges.append((0, 6, bridge_weight))\n\n    weighted_degree = weights.sum(axis=0)\n    if clock_degree < float(weighted_degree.max()):\n        raise ValueError(\"The common clock must dominate every weighted degree.\")\n    laplacian = np.diag(weighted_degree) - weights\n    transition = np.eye(12) - laplacian / (2.0 * clock_degree)\n    stationary = np.full(12, 1.0 / 12)\n    assert np.allclose(transition, transition.T)\n    assert np.allclose(transition.sum(axis=0), 1.0)\n    assert np.all(transition >= 0)\n    assert np.all(np.diag(transition) >= 0.5 - 1e-14)\n    assert np.allclose(transition @ stationary, stationary)\n    return {\n        \"weights\": weights,\n        \"edges\": tuple(edges),\n        \"degree\": weighted_degree,\n        \"laplacian\": laplacian,\n        \"P\": transition,\n        \"pi\": stationary,\n        \"bridge_weight\": bridge_weight,\n        \"clock_degree\": clock_degree,\n        \"stop_count\": 12,\n    }\n\n\nspectral_seed = 314159\nspectral_checkpoints = (100, 1000, 10000)\nspectral_start_pair = (3, 9)\nspectral_bridge_weights = {\"weak\": 0.15, \"strong\": 1.0}\n# The strongest graph has maximum weighted degree 2 + 1 = 3.\nspectral_common_clock = 3.0\nsingle_models = {\n    name: build_stop_graph(weight, spectral_common_clock)\n    for name, weight in spectral_bridge_weights.items()\n}\nstop_labels = tuple(\"Bay \" + str(index) for index in range(12))"
      },
      {
        "type": "code",
        "language": "python",
        "name": "draw_routes",
        "code": "# A fixed, explicit palette keeps these charts readable in either app theme.\nplot_background = \"#1c2128\"\nplot_ink = \"#e6edf3\"\nplot_colours = {\"weak\": \"#fbbf24\", \"strong\": \"#63c5ff\"}\n\n\ndef spectral_layout(title, height=420, **extra):\n    \"\"\"Use short two-line titles and a horizontal legend on narrow screens.\"\"\"\n    layout = {\n        \"height\": height,\n        \"margin\": {\"t\": 76, \"b\": 100, \"l\": 62, \"r\": 16},\n        \"title\": {\"text\": title, \"x\": 0.5, \"font\": {\"size\": 14}},\n        \"paper_bgcolor\": plot_background,\n        \"plot_bgcolor\": plot_background,\n        \"font\": {\"size\": 11, \"color\": plot_ink},\n        \"legend\": {\"orientation\": \"h\", \"x\": 0, \"y\": -0.26, \"font\": {\"size\": 10}},\n        \"xaxis\": {\"automargin\": True},\n        \"yaxis\": {\"automargin\": True},\n    }\n    layout.update(extra)\n    return layout\n\n\ndef show_pair_map(distribution, model, title, colour_limit, colour_label):\n    \"\"\"Plot 132 legal tiles using bars, supported by SciREPL's Plotly Basic.\n\n    Diagonal bays are omitted. Legal zero values remain actual colour data.\n    x=A's stop; y=B's stop. There are 13 boundaries around 12 tiles.\n    \"\"\"\n    grid = pair_distribution_grid(distribution, model)\n    rows, columns = np.nonzero(joint_legal_mask)\n    values = grid[rows, columns]\n    assert np.all(np.isfinite(values)) and np.all(values >= -1e-12)\n    values = np.maximum(values, 0)\n    labels = [\"A=\" + str(column) + \", B=\" + str(row) + \": \" + format(float(value), \".6f\")\n              for row, column, value in zip(rows, columns, values)]\n    borders = ([{\"type\": \"line\", \"x0\": edge - 0.5, \"x1\": edge - 0.5,\n                 \"y0\": -0.5, \"y1\": 11.5, \"layer\": \"above\",\n                 \"line\": {\"color\": \"rgba(230,237,243,0.3)\", \"width\": 0.5}}\n                for edge in range(13)]\n               + [{\"type\": \"line\", \"y0\": edge - 0.5, \"y1\": edge - 0.5,\n                   \"x0\": -0.5, \"x1\": 11.5, \"layer\": \"above\",\n                   \"line\": {\"color\": \"rgba(230,237,243,0.3)\", \"width\": 0.5}}\n                  for edge in range(13)])\n    axis = {\"range\": [-0.5, 11.5], \"showgrid\": False, \"zeroline\": False,\n            \"tickmode\": \"array\", \"tickvals\": list(range(12)), \"automargin\": True}\n    layout = spectral_layout(title, height=460, showlegend=False,\n                             barmode=\"overlay\", bargap=0, shapes=borders,\n                             xaxis=axis, yaxis=dict(axis))\n    layout[\"plot_bgcolor\"] = \"#0b0e12\"\n    layout[\"margin\"] = {\"t\": 76, \"b\": 140, \"l\": 65, \"r\": 12}\n    mplot([{\"type\": \"bar\", \"x\": columns.tolist(), \"y\": [1] * len(rows),\n            \"base\": (rows - 0.5).tolist(), \"width\": 1, \"text\": labels,\n            \"hoverinfo\": \"text\", \"textposition\": \"none\",\n            \"marker\": {\"color\": values.tolist(), \"colorscale\": \"Viridis\",\n                       \"cmin\": 0, \"cmax\": float(colour_limit), \"showscale\": True,\n                       \"line\": {\"width\": 0}, \"colorbar\": {\n                           \"orientation\": \"h\", \"x\": 0.5, \"y\": -0.34,\n                           \"xanchor\": \"center\", \"yanchor\": \"top\", \"len\": 0.9,\n                           \"thickness\": 12, \"tickformat\": \".3f\",\n                           \"title\": {\"text\": colour_label, \"side\": \"top\"}}}}],\n          title=title, xlabel=\"Truck A stop · 0–11\", ylabel=\"Truck B stop · 0–11\", layout=layout)\n\n\nangles = 2 * np.pi * np.arange(6) / 6\nroute_x = np.concatenate((-1.6 + 0.9 * np.cos(angles), 1.6 - 0.9 * np.cos(angles)))\nroute_y = np.concatenate((0.9 * np.sin(angles), 0.9 * np.sin(angles)))\nlocal_edge_x, local_edge_y = [], []\nfor first, second, weight in single_models[\"weak\"][\"edges\"][:-1]:\n    local_edge_x.extend([float(route_x[first]), float(route_x[second]), None])\n    local_edge_y.extend([float(route_y[first]), float(route_y[second]), None])\nroute_title = \"Two neighbourhoods<br>bridge 0 ↔ 6\"\nmplot([\n    {\"x\": local_edge_x, \"y\": local_edge_y, \"mode\": \"lines\", \"name\": \"Local edge\",\n     \"line\": {\"color\": \"#8596a7\", \"width\": 2}},\n    {\"x\": [float(route_x[0]), float(route_x[6])], \"y\": [0, 0], \"mode\": \"lines\",\n     \"name\": \"Bridge\", \"line\": {\"color\": \"#fbbf24\", \"width\": 4}},\n    {\"x\": route_x.tolist(), \"y\": route_y.tolist(), \"mode\": \"markers+text\",\n     \"text\": [str(stop) for stop in range(12)], \"textposition\": \"top center\",\n     \"name\": \"Loading bay\", \"marker\": {\"size\": 11, \"color\": \"#63c5ff\"}},\n], title=route_title, xlabel=\"West: 0–5 · East: 6–11\", ylabel=\"\",\n    layout=spectral_layout(route_title, height=360,\n                           xaxis={\"showgrid\": False, \"zeroline\": False, \"showticklabels\": False, \"range\": [-2.9, 2.9]},\n                           yaxis={\"showgrid\": False, \"zeroline\": False, \"showticklabels\": False, \"range\": [-1.4, 1.4]}))\nprint(\"Waiting probabilities are not drawn as loops.\")"
      },
      {
        "type": "markdown",
        "language": "markdown",
        "name": "single_bridge",
        "code": "## 2 · Endpoint probability is not a visit histogram\n\nThe one-truck experiment starts at **stop 3**. Its one-hot column vector is $e_3$: entry 3 is 1 and every other entry is 0. After exactly $t$ moves,\n\n$$q_t=P^t e_3.$$\n\nEntry $d$ of $q_t$ is the probability that the truck is at stop $d$ **at that time**. It is not the fraction of earlier visits to $d$. The following code propagates the probability vector directly, without sampling a route.\n\nWe measure distance from stationarity with **total variation**:\n\n$$\\mathrm{TV}(q,\\pi)=\\frac12\\sum_j|q_j-\\pi_j|.$$\n\nZero means the distributions match. The stronger bridge speeds exchange between neighbourhoods. The bar chart at $t=100$ shows the actual endpoint distributions, not a running average. A single truck's eventual target is $1/12$ per stop."
      },
      {
        "type": "code",
        "language": "python",
        "name": "single_endpoints",
        "code": "single_times = np.arange(301)\nsingle_endpoint_curves = {}\nsingle_endpoint_100 = {}\nfor case, model in single_models.items():\n    probability = np.zeros(12)\n    probability[3] = 1\n    distances = []\n    for step in single_times:\n        distances.append(float(0.5 * np.abs(probability - model[\"pi\"]).sum()))\n        if step == 100:\n            single_endpoint_100[case] = probability.copy()\n        probability = model[\"P\"] @ probability\n    single_endpoint_curves[case] = distances\n\nsingle_title = \"One truck's endpoint<br>start at stop 3\"\nmplot([{\"x\": single_times.tolist(), \"y\": values, \"name\": case,\n        \"line\": {\"color\": plot_colours[case]}}\n       for case, values in single_endpoint_curves.items()],\n      title=single_title, xlabel=\"Moves t\", ylabel=\"Total variation\",\n      layout=spectral_layout(single_title))\nsingle_bar_title = \"Where is the truck?<br>Endpoint at t = 100\"\nmplot([{\"type\": \"bar\", \"x\": list(range(12)), \"y\": values.tolist(), \"name\": case,\n        \"marker\": {\"color\": plot_colours[case]}}\n       for case, values in single_endpoint_100.items()]\n      + [{\"x\": list(range(12)), \"y\": [1 / 12] * 12, \"name\": \"Uniform target\",\n          \"line\": {\"color\": \"#e6edf3\", \"dash\": \"dot\"}}],\n      title=single_bar_title, xlabel=\"Stop index · 0–11\", ylabel=\"Endpoint probability\",\n      layout=spectral_layout(single_bar_title, barmode=\"group\",\n                             xaxis={\"tickmode\": \"array\", \"tickvals\": list(range(12))}))"
      },
      {
        "type": "markdown",
        "language": "markdown",
        "name": "joint_bridge",
        "code": "## 3 · Two trucks make a 12 × 12 map\n\nA state is the **ordered pair** $(a,b)$: truck A at stop $a$, truck B at stop $b$. Swapping the two labels gives a different state. At each step:\n\n1. Choose A or B with probability $1/2$.\n2. Propose a move using that truck's source column of the one-truck matrix $P$.\n3. If its proposed bay is occupied by the other truck, **stay instead**. Keep this probability; do not discard it and renormalize the remaining moves.\n\nThe other truck stays put. Ordinary waiting and rejected moves both count as full time steps. A rejected proposal is **not rerouted immediately**: the truck waits until a later step.\n\n**What are the routing rules?** The road graph is fixed, but a truck's route is a fresh sequence of random choices. Trucks have no delivery list, preferred destination, timetable or memory of earlier stops. They do not optimize a route or try to visit every stop once: this is **not a travelling-salesman problem**. A reverse move on the next step is allowed.\n\n**Are closer routes preferred? Is there a warehouse?** Each move goes only to an adjacent stop, so the model is local. Both within-neighbourhood directions have equal probability; no distances or travel times are supplied, and the picture's spacing is only a drawing. There is no designated warehouse or scheduled return. All bays have the same role. A future warehouse-return experiment would need to distinguish elapsed abstract steps, actual moves, and completed stops: waiting at the warehouse would otherwise count as an immediate return.\n\n**Only bay congestion is modeled:** each bay holds one truck. Roads have no queues, travel times, lane capacities or occupancy, and trucks do not learn to avoid traffic. A narrow-looking bridge stands for an externally chosen low crossing probability. Interacting trucks are still described by a fixed matrix on the full pair state; their joint law evolves linearly as $q_{t+1}=P_{\\rm pair}q_t$. A traffic model that changes routing probabilities with an evolving aggregate congestion distribution would be a different, generally nonlinear model.\n\nThere are $12\\times11=132$ legal ordered pairs. The code discovers them by breadth-first search and verifies that every pair with $a\\ne b$ is reachable. The resulting $132\\times132$ transition matrix $P_{\\rm pair}$ remains symmetric and lazy, with stationary probability $1/132$ per legal pair.\n\n**Read the heatmap by its axes, not by colour changes:** horizontal position is **truck A's stop (0–11)**; vertical position is **truck B's stop (0–11)**. There are 12 tiles per axis, bounded by 13 grid lines. The 12 dark diagonal tiles are forbidden shared bays. Every off-diagonal tile is legal—even if a finite route has not visited it. A legal zero has the colour-scale's zero colour, not the dark forbidden background.\n\nThis is an exclusion model chosen for teaching. Separate loading bays at a real depot, traffic queues, route schedules and vehicle capacities would need different rules."
      },
      {
        "type": "code",
        "language": "python",
        "name": "joint_kernel",
        "code": "\"\"\"Cell 2: labelled trucks A/B with one truck per loading bay.\n\nA step chooses A or B with probability 1/2 and proposes a single-truck\nP move.  A proposal to the other truck's bay becomes a stay: it is NOT\ndiscarded and the remaining proposals are NOT renormalized.\n\"\"\"\n\n\ndef joint_successors(pair, base_transition):\n    \"\"\"List (destination_pair, probability) proposals, including rejected stays.\n\n    Duplicate destinations are intentional; the joint matrix accumulates\n    them, including the two kinds of ordinary stay and occupied-bay rejection.\n    \"\"\"\n    first, second = pair\n    successors = []\n    for truck_index in (0, 1):\n        source_stop = pair[truck_index]\n        other_stop = pair[1 - truck_index]\n        for destination_stop in range(base_transition.shape[0]):\n            proposal_probability = float(base_transition[destination_stop, source_stop])\n            if proposal_probability <= 0:\n                continue\n            destination = list(pair)\n            if destination_stop != other_stop:\n                destination[truck_index] = destination_stop\n            successors.append((tuple(destination), 0.5 * proposal_probability))\n    return successors\n\n\ndef build_joint_model(single_model, start_pair=(3, 9)):\n    \"\"\"Discover the legal communicating class by BFS, then build its kernel.\n\n    We verify 12*11 reachable ordered pairs after discovery, instead of\n    assuming that exclusion leaves every off-diagonal pair reachable.\n    \"\"\"\n    base_transition = np.asarray(single_model[\"P\"], dtype=float)\n    stop_count = base_transition.shape[0]\n    start_pair = tuple(int(stop) for stop in start_pair)\n    if len(start_pair) != 2 or start_pair[0] == start_pair[1]:\n        raise ValueError(\"A and B must start at two different bays.\")\n    if any(stop < 0 or stop >= stop_count for stop in start_pair):\n        raise ValueError(\"The starting bay is outside the graph.\")\n\n    queue = deque([start_pair])\n    discovered = {start_pair}\n    states = []\n    while queue:\n        pair = queue.popleft()\n        states.append(pair)\n        for destination, probability in joint_successors(pair, base_transition):\n            if probability > 0 and destination not in discovered:\n                discovered.add(destination)\n                queue.append(destination)\n    state_index = {pair: index for index, pair in enumerate(states)}\n    expected_legal = {\n        (first, second)\n        for first in range(stop_count)\n        for second in range(stop_count)\n        if first != second\n    }\n    assert discovered == expected_legal\n    assert len(states) == stop_count * (stop_count - 1) == 132\n\n    transition = np.zeros((len(states), len(states)), dtype=float)\n    for source_index, pair in enumerate(states):\n        for destination, probability in joint_successors(pair, base_transition):\n            transition[state_index[destination], source_index] += probability\n    stationary = np.full(len(states), 1.0 / len(states))\n    assert np.allclose(transition, transition.T)\n    assert np.allclose(transition.sum(axis=0), 1.0)\n    assert np.all(transition >= 0)\n    assert np.all(np.diag(transition) >= 0.5 - 1e-14)\n    assert np.allclose(transition @ stationary, stationary)\n    return {\n        \"P\": transition,\n        \"pi\": stationary,\n        \"states\": tuple(states),\n        \"state_index\": state_index,\n        \"start_pair\": start_pair,\n        \"start_index\": state_index[start_pair],\n        \"stop_count\": stop_count,\n        \"bridge_weight\": single_model[\"bridge_weight\"],\n        \"clock_degree\": single_model[\"clock_degree\"],\n    }\n\n\ndef pair_distribution_grid(values, model, forbidden_value=np.nan):\n    \"\"\"Map a state vector to a 12x12 chart: x=A bay, y=B bay.\n\n    A forbidden diagonal is NaN by default; off-diagonal zero entries remain\n    actual zeros so an unvisited legal pair is not confused with an illegal one.\n    \"\"\"\n    values = np.asarray(values)\n    if values.shape != (len(model[\"states\"]),):\n        raise ValueError(\"One value is required per reachable ordered pair.\")\n    grid = np.full((model[\"stop_count\"], model[\"stop_count\"]), forbidden_value, dtype=float)\n    for state, (first, second) in enumerate(model[\"states\"]):\n        grid[second, first] = values[state]\n    return grid\n\n\ndef pair_distribution_marginals(values, model):\n    \"\"\"Return A and B bay marginals without interpreting forbidden cells as data.\"\"\"\n    grid = pair_distribution_grid(values, model, forbidden_value=0.0)\n    return grid.sum(axis=0), grid.sum(axis=1)\n\n\ndef pooled_stop_distribution(values, model):\n    \"\"\"Pool the labelled A/B marginals, intentionally forgetting which truck.\n\n    A uniform pooled distribution does not establish uniformity of either\n    label or of the full ordered pair; opposite label imbalances can cancel.\n    \"\"\"\n    first_marginal, second_marginal = pair_distribution_marginals(values, model)\n    return 0.5 * (first_marginal + second_marginal)\n\n\njoint_models = {\n    name: build_joint_model(model, spectral_start_pair)\n    for name, model in single_models.items()\n}\njoint_legal_mask = ~np.eye(12, dtype=bool)\njoint_uniform_target = 1.0 / 132"
      },
      {
        "type": "markdown",
        "language": "markdown",
        "name": "spectrum_bridge",
        "code": "## 4 · Slow graph patterns, not ordinary image frequencies\n\nA heatmap's nearby pixels need not be nearby **states of the routing graph**. Relabelling the stops rearranges the picture without changing the chain. Graph eigenvectors give patterns adapted to allowed transitions, including the forbidden diagonal.\n\nFor either model, let $M$ be its number of states (12 or 132). A nonconstant mode satisfies\n\n$$P u_k=\\rho_k u_k,\\qquad \\mu_k=1-\\rho_k.$$\n\nHere $\\mu_k$ is an eigenvalue of $I-P$; for the one-truck model, $I-P=L/6$. Our lazy symmetric matrices have $0\\le\\rho_k<1$ for nonconstant modes. After $t$ moves, an endpoint coefficient is multiplied by $\\rho_k^t$. A small $\\mu_k$ therefore means slow decay. The **spectral gap** is the smallest nonzero $\\mu_k$; its reciprocal is a relaxation-time scale, not a guarantee of a particular total-variation tolerance.\n\nThe next helper cell diagonalizes both models. It uses $\\phi_k=\\sqrt M\\,u_k$, so nonconstant modes have mean zero and variance one under the uniform stationary distribution. This normalization matters for the later uncertainty formulas.\n\nThe spectrum plot shows that the two-truck problem contains slower modes than one-truck routing. **Compare the clocks carefully:** a pair step selects only one truck, so each label receives a proposal on average once every two steps. Comparing one-truck and pair spectra includes this slower per-truck clock as well as joint-state constraints. Weak-versus-strong comparisons within either model keep the same clock.\n\nWithin the pair model, keeping track of **which truck** crossed the bottleneck can be slower than balancing their combined stop counts. These are graph modes; we do not estimate graph dimensionality from this tiny example."
      },
      {
        "type": "code",
        "language": "python",
        "name": "spectral_tools",
        "code": "\"\"\"Cell 3: reversible modes, finite-record uncertainty, and exact model means.\n\nHere \"exact\" means a deterministic finite-state calculation, evaluated in\nfloating point, rather than a Monte Carlo estimate.  Occupancy records count\nX_1,...,X_n AFTER the first move; an endpoint is the law of X_n alone.\n\"\"\"\n\n\ndef uniform_eigensystem(transition):\n    \"\"\"Diagonalize a symmetric lazy kernel with uniform-pi normalized modes.\n\n    psi_j = sqrt(m) v_j has E_pi[psi_j psi_k] = delta_jk.  The first\n    mode is the constant 1 and the remaining modes have zero stationary mean.\n    A sign convention helps reproducibility for a SIMPLE eigenvalue; vectors\n    inside repeated eigenspaces are still arbitrary, so aggregate/projector\n    quantities should be used when displaying a degenerate band.\n    \"\"\"\n    transition = np.asarray(transition, dtype=float)\n    if transition.ndim != 2 or transition.shape[0] != transition.shape[1]:\n        raise ValueError(\"The transition matrix must be square.\")\n    if not np.allclose(transition, transition.T):\n        raise ValueError(\"Uniform reversible modes require a symmetric kernel.\")\n    eigenvalues, vectors = np.linalg.eigh(transition)\n    order = np.argsort(eigenvalues)[::-1]\n    eigenvalues = eigenvalues[order]\n    vectors = vectors[:, order]\n    assert np.allclose(transition.sum(axis=0), 1.0)\n    assert eigenvalues[-1] >= -1e-12\n    assert np.isclose(eigenvalues[0], 1.0)\n    assert eigenvalues[1] < 1.0 - 1e-12\n    # Remove tiny roundoff below zero or above one, not negative model modes.\n    eigenvalues = np.clip(eigenvalues, 0.0, 1.0)\n    eigenvalues[0] = 1.0\n    for mode in range(vectors.shape[1]):\n        anchor = int(np.argmax(np.abs(vectors[:, mode])))\n        if vectors[anchor, mode] < 0:\n            vectors[:, mode] *= -1.0\n    # The stationary vector is known analytically and avoids sign/roundoff noise.\n    vectors[:, 0] = 1.0 / np.sqrt(transition.shape[0])\n    functions = np.sqrt(transition.shape[0]) * vectors\n    assert np.allclose(vectors.T @ vectors, np.eye(transition.shape[0]), atol=1e-11)\n    assert np.allclose(transition @ vectors, vectors * eigenvalues, atol=1e-11)\n    assert np.allclose(functions[:, 1:].mean(axis=0), 0.0, atol=1e-11)\n    return {\n        \"eigenvalues\": eigenvalues,\n        \"vectors\": vectors,\n        \"psi\": functions,\n        \"gap\": float(1.0 - eigenvalues[1]),\n        \"mode_count\": transition.shape[0] - 1,\n    }\n\n\ndef _positive_step_count(step_count):\n    if isinstance(step_count, (bool, np.bool_)) or int(step_count) != step_count or step_count < 1:\n        raise ValueError(\"The number of recorded moves must be a positive integer.\")\n    return int(step_count)\n\n\ndef finite_n_tau(eigenvalues, step_count):\n    \"\"\"Stable tau_n = 1 + 2 sum_{k=1}^{n-1}(1-k/n) rho**k.\n\n    For a stationary, unit-variance eigenfunction, Var(mean_n) = tau_n/n.\n    Weighted recurrence avoids cancellation of the closed form when rho is\n    near 1.  This is the finite-n value, not (1+rho)/(1-rho).\n    \"\"\"\n    step_count = _positive_step_count(step_count)\n    eigenvalues = np.asarray(eigenvalues, dtype=float)\n    if np.any(~np.isfinite(eigenvalues)) or np.any(eigenvalues < 0) or np.any(eigenvalues > 1):\n        raise ValueError(\"This lazy-model formula expects eigenvalues in [0, 1].\")\n    result = np.ones_like(eigenvalues)\n    power = np.ones_like(eigenvalues)\n    for lag in range(1, step_count):\n        power *= eigenvalues\n        result += 2.0 * (1.0 - lag / step_count) * power\n    return result\n\n\ndef first_move_geometric_average(eigenvalues, step_count):\n    \"\"\"Return n^-1 sum_{t=1}^n rho**t, stable for rho close to 1.\n\n    The leading rho matters: counting X_0,...,X_(n-1) would be a different\n    estimator.  expm1 preserves the small numerator near a slow eigenvalue.\n    \"\"\"\n    step_count = _positive_step_count(step_count)\n    eigenvalues = np.asarray(eigenvalues, dtype=float)\n    if np.any(eigenvalues < 0) or np.any(eigenvalues > 1) or np.any(~np.isfinite(eigenvalues)):\n        raise ValueError(\"This lazy-model formula expects eigenvalues in [0, 1].\")\n    result = np.zeros_like(eigenvalues)\n    result[eigenvalues == 1] = 1.0\n    interior = (eigenvalues > 0) & (eigenvalues < 1)\n    rho = eigenvalues[interior]\n    result[interior] = rho * (-np.expm1(step_count * np.log(rho))) / (step_count * (1.0 - rho))\n    return result\n\n\ndef total_variation(distribution, stationary):\n    \"\"\"Raw probability distance; it does not account for serial correlation.\"\"\"\n    distribution = np.asarray(distribution, dtype=float)\n    stationary = np.asarray(stationary, dtype=float)\n    if distribution.shape != stationary.shape:\n        raise ValueError(\"The distributions must have matching shapes.\")\n    return float(0.5 * np.abs(distribution - stationary).sum())\n\n\ndef spectral_whitening(distribution, eigensystem, step_count):\n    \"\"\"Normalize occupancy-mode energy by its STATIONARY finite-n variance.\n\n    W = sum_j n * mean(psi_j)**2 / tau_n,j / (m-1).  Stationary starts\n    have E[W]=1 exactly; W is not a proof of mixing or a chi-square test.\n    The fixed starts used in the main experiment can have bias and different\n    variances.  Equal-eigenvalue rotations leave the aggregate W unchanged.\n    \"\"\"\n    step_count = _positive_step_count(step_count)\n    distribution = np.asarray(distribution, dtype=float)\n    functions = eigensystem[\"psi\"][:, 1:]\n    if distribution.shape != (functions.shape[0],):\n        raise ValueError(\"One occupancy probability is required per state.\")\n    mode_means = distribution @ functions\n    tau = finite_n_tau(eigensystem[\"eigenvalues\"][1:], step_count)\n    mode_energy = step_count * mode_means**2 / tau\n    return {\n        \"W\": float(mode_energy.mean()),\n        \"energy_sum\": float(mode_energy.sum()),\n        \"mode_means\": mode_means,\n        \"mode_energy\": mode_energy,\n        \"tau\": tau,\n        \"stationary_expected_W\": 1.0,\n    }\n\n\ndef exact_endpoint_distribution(transition, start_index, step_count):\n    \"\"\"Compute P**n e_start, not an empirical occupation histogram.\"\"\"\n    if isinstance(step_count, (bool, np.bool_)) or int(step_count) != step_count or step_count < 0:\n        raise ValueError(\"The endpoint step must be a nonnegative integer.\")\n    start = np.zeros(transition.shape[0])\n    start[int(start_index)] = 1.0\n    return np.linalg.matrix_power(transition, int(step_count)) @ start\n\n\ndef exact_occupation_expectation(eigensystem, start_index, step_count):\n    \"\"\"Expected counts, mean histogram, and fixed-start bias after n moves.\n\n    E[hat_pi_n] = sum_j v_j v_j(start) * (sum_{t=1}^n rho_j**t)/n.\n    All nonconstant terms are fixed-start bias, not sampling fluctuation.\n    \"\"\"\n    step_count = _positive_step_count(step_count)\n    vectors = eigensystem[\"vectors\"]\n    average_power = first_move_geometric_average(eigensystem[\"eigenvalues\"], step_count)\n    expected_distribution = vectors @ (average_power * vectors[int(start_index), :])\n    stationary = np.full(vectors.shape[0], 1.0 / vectors.shape[0])\n    expected_mode_means = average_power[1:] * eigensystem[\"psi\"][int(start_index), 1:]\n    assert np.isclose(expected_distribution.sum(), 1.0)\n    assert np.all(expected_distribution >= -1e-12)\n    return {\n        \"counts\": step_count * expected_distribution,\n        \"distribution\": expected_distribution,\n        \"bias\": expected_distribution - stationary,\n        \"bias_tv\": total_variation(expected_distribution, stationary),\n        \"mode_bias\": expected_mode_means,\n    }\n\n\ndef eigenvalue_bands(eigensystem, tolerance=1e-10):\n    \"\"\"Group nonconstant equal-eigenvalue modes for basis-invariant displays.\n\n    A band's squared projected coordinate at each state is invariant under\n    rotation of a degenerate eigenbasis.  Mode indices refer to full psi\n    columns, so 0 (the stationary constant) is never included.\n    \"\"\"\n    eigenvalues = eigensystem[\"eigenvalues\"]\n    bands = []\n    first = 1\n    while first < len(eigenvalues):\n        last = first + 1\n        while last < len(eigenvalues) and abs(eigenvalues[last] - eigenvalues[first]) <= tolerance:\n            last += 1\n        indices = tuple(range(first, last))\n        band_functions = eigensystem[\"psi\"][:, first:last]\n        bands.append({\n            \"indices\": indices,\n            \"eigenvalue\": float(eigenvalues[first:last].mean()),\n            \"multiplicity\": last - first,\n            \"state_energy\": np.sum(band_functions**2, axis=1),\n        })\n        first = last\n    return tuple(bands)\n\n\nsingle_spectra = {name: uniform_eigensystem(model[\"P\"]) for name, model in single_models.items()}\njoint_spectra = {name: uniform_eigensystem(model[\"P\"]) for name, model in joint_models.items()}\njoint_spectral_bands = {name: eigenvalue_bands(spectrum) for name, spectrum in joint_spectra.items()}"
      },
      {
        "type": "code",
        "language": "python",
        "name": "spectral_picture",
        "code": "spectrum_title = \"Slow decay rates<br>μ = 1 − ρ\"\nspectrum_traces = []\nfor case in single_models:\n    for kind, spectra, dash in ((\"one\", single_spectra, \"dot\"), (\"pair\", joint_spectra, \"solid\")):\n        rates = 1 - spectra[case][\"eigenvalues\"][1:]\n        spectrum_traces.append({\"x\": list(range(1, min(12, len(rates)) + 1)),\n                                \"y\": rates[:12].tolist(), \"name\": case + \" · \" + kind,\n                                \"mode\": \"lines+markers\", \"line\": {\"color\": plot_colours[case], \"dash\": dash}})\n        print(case, kind, \"gap:\", round(spectra[case][\"gap\"], 8))\nmplot(spectrum_traces, title=spectrum_title, xlabel=\"Nonconstant mode rank\", ylabel=\"Decay rate μ\",\n      layout=spectral_layout(spectrum_title, yaxis={\"type\": \"log\", \"automargin\": True}))\n# The slowest pair eigenvalue is simple. Sign flips change neither this\n# exchange relation nor the squared errors used in later plots.\nfor case, model in joint_models.items():\n    slow = joint_spectra[case][\"psi\"][:, 1]\n    swapped = np.array([slow[model[\"state_index\"][(b, a)]] for a, b in model[\"states\"]])\n    assert np.allclose(swapped, -slow, atol=1e-10)\nprint(\"The slowest pair mode changes sign when truck labels A/B are swapped.\")"
      },
      {
        "type": "markdown",
        "language": "markdown",
        "name": "endpoint_bridge",
        "code": "## 5 · Balanced pooled visits can hide an unmixed pair\n\nStart at $(3,9)$: A west of the bridge and B east of it, at opposite points of their cycles. The two heatmaps show the **exact endpoint law at step 100**, computed from $P_{\\rm pair}^{100}$ rather than a sampled route. Both use the same colour scale. At stationarity every off-diagonal tile would have probability $1/132\\approx0.00758$.\n\nFor each heatmap, summing a column gives A's marginal probability and summing a row gives B's. **Pooling** averages these two marginals and deliberately forgets the truck label:\n\n$$q_{\\rm pooled}(j)=\\frac12\\{\\Pr(A=j)+\\Pr(B=j)\\}.$$\n\nAt step 100, pooled distance from $1/12$ is about **0.000111** for both bridge weights. Yet joint distance from $1/132$ is about **0.521** (weak) or **0.291** (strong). A can remain disproportionately west while B remains disproportionately east; those opposite label imbalances nearly cancel when pooled. **Neither individual marginal is thereby proven uniform.**\n\nThe curve plot compares joint and pooled endpoint distances over time. A balanced aggregate statistic can miss a slowly relaxing pattern. This is why a white-looking heatmap, or a good one-dimensional marginal, is not a general mixing test.\n\n**Fast locally, slow globally:** probability can redistribute within each neighbourhood before much crosses the bridge. Removing a neighbourhood imbalance requires enough probability flow across that cut—not that every individual route has crossed by a particular time. Late error is dominated by slow modes only if the initial distribution excites them. Pooling A/B can cancel the slow labelled-exchange pattern and hide it."
      },
      {
        "type": "code",
        "language": "python",
        "name": "joint_endpoints",
        "code": "joint_endpoint_100 = {case: exact_endpoint_distribution(model[\"P\"], model[\"start_index\"], 100)\n                      for case, model in joint_models.items()}\nendpoint_colour_limit = max(float(values.max()) for values in joint_endpoint_100.values())\nfor case, model in joint_models.items():\n    probability = joint_endpoint_100[case]\n    show_pair_map(probability, model, case + \" bridge · endpoint<br>t = 100 · 12 × 12\",\n                  endpoint_colour_limit, \"Endpoint probability\")\n    marginal_a, marginal_b = pair_distribution_marginals(probability, model)\n    pooled = pooled_stop_distribution(probability, model)\n    print(case, \"joint TV:\", round(total_variation(probability, model[\"pi\"]), 6),\n          \"pooled TV:\", round(total_variation(pooled, np.full(12, 1 / 12)), 6))\n    print(\"Individual marginal TV, A/B:\",\n          round(total_variation(marginal_a, np.full(12, 1 / 12)), 6),\n          round(total_variation(marginal_b, np.full(12, 1 / 12)), 6))\n\njoint_curve_times = [1, 3, 10, 30, 100, 300, 1000, 3000]\njoint_endpoint_curve_traces = []\nfor case, model in joint_models.items():\n    vectors = joint_spectra[case][\"vectors\"]\n    eigenvalues = joint_spectra[case][\"eigenvalues\"]\n    source = vectors[model[\"start_index\"], :]\n    joint_distances, pooled_distances = [], []\n    for step in joint_curve_times:\n        probability = vectors @ (source * eigenvalues**step)\n        joint_distances.append(total_variation(probability, model[\"pi\"]))\n        pooled_distances.append(total_variation(pooled_stop_distribution(probability, model), np.full(12, 1 / 12)))\n    for kind, values, dash in ((\"joint\", joint_distances, \"solid\"), (\"pooled\", pooled_distances, \"dot\")):\n        joint_endpoint_curve_traces.append({\"x\": joint_curve_times, \"y\": values, \"name\": case + \" · \" + kind,\n                                           \"mode\": \"lines+markers\", \"line\": {\"color\": plot_colours[case], \"dash\": dash}})\njoint_curve_title = \"Joint versus pooled<br>exact endpoint distance\"\nmplot(joint_endpoint_curve_traces, title=joint_curve_title, xlabel=\"Moves t · log scale\", ylabel=\"Total variation\",\n      layout=spectral_layout(joint_curve_title, xaxis={\"type\": \"log\", \"automargin\": True}))"
      },
      {
        "type": "markdown",
        "language": "markdown",
        "name": "entropy_bridge",
        "code": "### 5a · Counting effective possibilities\n\nBoth bridge cases have the same legal states and allowed routes, but different route probabilities. A raw count treats very unlikely outcomes like likely ones. For the **exact endpoint distribution** $q_t$, entropy provides a probability-weighted effective count:\n\n$$H_t=-\\sum_s q_t(s)\\log_2 q_t(s),\\qquad N_{\\rm eff}(t)=2^{H_t}.$$\n\nTerms with zero probability contribute zero. If $K$ outcomes are equally likely, $N_{\\rm eff}=K$. Two outcomes with probabilities 99% and 1% give $N_{\\rm eff}\\approx1.06$, not 2. Rare outcomes are not deleted; many rare outcomes together can still carry important probability.\n\nFrom the known starting pair, $H_0=0$. After one move, **five** endpoint pairs are possible, but their effective count is only **three**: waiting has probability $2/3$, and each of four neighbouring pairs has probability $1/12$. At uniform equilibrium, $H=\\log_2 132\\approx7.044$ bits and $N_{\\rm eff}=132$.\n\nFor this uniform target, the entropy deficit is exactly KL divergence in bits:\n\n$$D_{\\rm KL,2}(q_t\\|\\pi)=\\log_2 132-H_t.$$\n\nThus every endpoint-entropy increase is an equal decrease in that distance from equilibrium. Its rate of increase is progress, not a fixed mixing-rate constant; close to equilibrium there is little deficit left to remove. For a **nonuniform** stationary target, use KL divergence to that target instead: Shannon entropy alone need not increase toward equilibrium.\n\n**State possibilities are not route histories.** New next-state choices can keep adding histories after the current-state distribution is already stationary. Their conditional entropy $H(X_{t+1}\\mid X_t)$ is not the same as the gain $H_{t+1}-H_t$: different histories merge at the same endpoint. Here a history records the pair state after each step, not hidden dispatcher decisions. Different truck selections or blocked proposals that produce the same stay are combined into one next-state outcome. Exactly,\n\n$$H_{t+1}-H_t=H(X_{t+1}\\mid X_t)-H(X_t\\mid X_{t+1}).$$\n\nThe first chart plots effective current-state possibilities. The second compares endpoint gain with new-choice entropy, both in bits per step. New choices remain uncertain after endpoint gain approaches zero. Both use propagated endpoint laws, **not entropy estimated from the noisy visit histogram**. [Entropy-rate background](https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/abs/10.1002/047174882X.ch4)\n\n**Network summaries differ:** destination Gini describes unequal visit totals; average degree counts connections; scale-free describes a degree-distribution shape. Neither destination inequality nor the number of links determines bottleneck strength. Our models have identical allowed edges and uniform stationary destination visits, yet their mixing rates differ. The graph's weighted arrangement matters."
      },
      {
        "type": "code",
        "language": "python",
        "name": "entropy_picture",
        "code": "def probability_entropy_bits(probability):\n    \"\"\"Shannon entropy of a normalized finite probability vector, in bits.\n\n    Ignore zero terms, not rare outcomes. These inputs are directly\n    propagated nonnegative laws; no histogram bias correction is applied.\n    \"\"\"\n    probability = np.asarray(probability, dtype=float)\n    if probability.ndim != 1 or len(probability) == 0:\n        raise ValueError(\"Entropy needs a nonempty probability vector.\")\n    if np.any(~np.isfinite(probability)) or np.any(probability < 0):\n        raise ValueError(\"Probabilities must be finite and nonnegative.\")\n    if not np.isclose(probability.sum(), 1.0, atol=1e-11, rtol=0):\n        raise ValueError(\"Probabilities must sum to one.\")\n    positive = probability > 0\n    return float(-np.sum(probability[positive] * np.log2(probability[positive])))\n\n\nentropy_horizon = 3000\nentropy_curves = {}\nentropy_snapshots = {}\nentropy_limit = float(np.log2(132))\nfor case, model in joint_models.items():\n    kernel = model[\"P\"]\n    # Each SOURCE column gives the distribution of the next full pair state.\n    choice_bits_by_state = np.array([probability_entropy_bits(kernel[:, source])\n                                     for source in range(len(model[\"states\"]))])\n    probability = np.zeros(len(model[\"states\"]))\n    probability[model[\"start_index\"]] = 1.0\n    entropies = [probability_entropy_bits(probability)]\n    choice_bits, gains = [], []\n    snapshots = {0: probability.copy()}\n    for step in range(1, entropy_horizon + 1):\n        # Conditional entropy averages source-column entropies under q_(t-1).\n        choice_bits.append(float(probability @ choice_bits_by_state))\n        probability = kernel @ probability\n        new_entropy = probability_entropy_bits(probability)\n        gains.append(new_entropy - entropies[-1])\n        entropies.append(new_entropy)\n        if step in joint_curve_times:\n            snapshots[step] = probability.copy()\n    entropy_curves[case] = {\n        \"state_bits\": np.array(entropies),\n        \"effective_count\": 2**np.array(entropies),\n        \"gain_bits\": np.array(gains),\n        \"new_choice_bits\": np.array(choice_bits),\n        \"stationary_choice_bits\": float(model[\"pi\"] @ choice_bits_by_state),\n    }\n    entropy_snapshots[case] = snapshots\n    assert np.min(gains) >= -1e-11\n    assert np.max(entropies) <= entropy_limit + 1e-11\n    assert np.isclose(entropies[1], np.log2(3))\n    assert np.count_nonzero(snapshots[1]) == 5\n    print(case, \"t=100; endpoint entropy:\", round(entropies[100], 6),\n          \"bits; effective pairs:\", round(float(2**entropies[100]), 6))\n    print(\"Stationary new-choice entropy:\",\n          round(entropy_curves[case][\"stationary_choice_bits\"], 6), \"bits per pair step\")\n\nentropy_count_title = \"Effective endpoint possibilities<br>uniform target: 132 pairs\"\nentropy_count_traces = [\n    {\"x\": joint_curve_times, \"y\": [float(curve[\"effective_count\"][step]) for step in joint_curve_times],\n     \"name\": case, \"mode\": \"lines+markers\", \"line\": {\"color\": plot_colours[case]}}\n    for case, curve in entropy_curves.items()\n]\nentropy_count_traces.append({\"x\": joint_curve_times, \"y\": [132] * len(joint_curve_times),\n                             \"name\": \"Uniform 132\", \"line\": {\"color\": plot_ink, \"dash\": \"dot\"}})\nmplot(entropy_count_traces, title=entropy_count_title, xlabel=\"Moves t · log scale\", ylabel=\"Effective pair count\",\n      layout=spectral_layout(entropy_count_title, xaxis={\"type\": \"log\", \"automargin\": True},\n                             yaxis={\"range\": [0, 140], \"automargin\": True}))\n\nentropy_rate_title = \"New choices versus state gain<br>bits per pair step\"\nentropy_rate_traces = []\nfor case, curve in entropy_curves.items():\n    for kind, field, dash in ((\"state gain\", \"gain_bits\", \"solid\"), (\"new choices\", \"new_choice_bits\", \"dot\")):\n        # At step t these arrays refer to the transition X_(t-1) -> X_t.\n        values = [max(0.0, float(curve[field][step - 1])) for step in joint_curve_times]\n        entropy_rate_traces.append({\"x\": joint_curve_times, \"y\": values, \"name\": case + \" · \" + kind,\n                                    \"mode\": \"lines+markers\", \"line\": {\"color\": plot_colours[case], \"dash\": dash}})\nmplot(entropy_rate_traces, title=entropy_rate_title, xlabel=\"Moves t · log scale\", ylabel=\"Bits per step\",\n      layout=spectral_layout(entropy_rate_title, xaxis={\"type\": \"log\", \"automargin\": True}))\nprint(\"Endpoint entropy gain can vanish while new route histories remain uncertain.\")\nprint(\"Uniform endpoint limit:\", round(entropy_limit, 6), \"bits.\")"
      },
      {
        "type": "markdown",
        "language": "markdown",
        "name": "clustering_bridge",
        "code": "### 5b · What does the pair map tell us about clustering?\n\nThe heatmap tells us the probability of each pair of stops. To summarize **clustering**, specify what \"near\" means: sharing a neighbourhood, occupying adjacent stops, or having a small graph distance. Joint total variation alone is not a clustering statistic.\n\nThe next cell uses **unweighted shortest-path distance**: each road counts as one hop, including the bridge. This is connectivity distance, not travel time or a distance penalized by the bridge's low crossing probability. Same-neighbourhood membership is a separate summary; stops 0 and 6 are adjacent even though they are in different neighbourhoods.\n\nCompare with the uniform distribution on the **132 legal ordered pairs**, not independent draws that would allow forbidden shared bays. At this equilibrium, 60 pairs share a neighbourhood, 26 are adjacent, and mean graph distance is 3:\n\n$$\\Pr(\\text{same neighbourhood})=\\frac{60}{132}=\\frac5{11},\\qquad\n\\Pr(\\text{adjacent})=\\frac{26}{132}=\\frac{13}{66}.$$\n\nStarting at $(3,9)$, the trucks initially occupy opposite neighbourhoods. At step 100, the same-neighbourhood probabilities are approximately **19.8%** (weak bridge) and **41.0%** (strong bridge), versus **45.5%** at equilibrium. These examples retain separation rather than demonstrating attraction or a traffic jam. They approach their baseline as the labelled pair mixes.\n\nReal congestion could add road occupancy, queues, destinations and route adaptation. Those variables are absent here. A fixed routing rule on an enlarged full state can still give a linear Markov update; rules depending on an evolving aggregate distribution can produce nonlinear equations. Pipe-flow and traffic models would be separate applications, not alternate interpretations of these toy probabilities."
      },
      {
        "type": "code",
        "language": "python",
        "name": "clustering_picture",
        "code": "\"\"\"Summaries of labelled-pair proximity, not truck counts or attraction.\n\nThe full pair law can show association even when pooled visits look uniform.\nThese descriptive summaries compare that law with the 132-state uniform\nexclusion reference; they do not assume the trucks are independent.\n\"\"\"\n\n\ndef unweighted_route_distances(weights):\n    \"\"\"Count shortest-path edges by BFS; weights do not become travel times.\n\n    Every positive-weight edge is one topological hop, including the bridge.\n    The weak and strong graphs have the same support, hence the same distances.\n    \"\"\"\n    adjacency = np.asarray(weights) > 0\n    stop_count = adjacency.shape[0]\n    if adjacency.shape != (stop_count, stop_count):\n        raise ValueError(\"Route weights must be a square matrix.\")\n    distances = np.full((stop_count, stop_count), -1, dtype=int)\n    for source in range(stop_count):\n        distances[source, source] = 0\n        pending = deque([source])\n        while pending:\n            current = pending.popleft()\n            for neighbour in np.flatnonzero(adjacency[:, current]):\n                if distances[source, neighbour] < 0:\n                    distances[source, neighbour] = distances[source, current] + 1\n                    pending.append(int(neighbour))\n    assert np.all(distances >= 0)\n    return distances\n\n\nroute_hop_distances = unweighted_route_distances(single_models[\"weak\"][\"weights\"])\npair_clustering_features = {}\njoint_clustering_curves = {}\njoint_clustering_at_100 = {}\nclustering_reference = None\n\nfor case, model in joint_models.items():\n    # Each feature is indexed by the model's BFS-ordered labelled states.\n    # Both endpoints in 0..5 or both in 6..11 means the same neighbourhood.\n    features = {\n        \"same_neighbourhood\": np.array([\n            (first < 6) == (second < 6) for first, second in model[\"states\"]\n        ], dtype=float),\n        \"adjacent\": np.array([\n            route_hop_distances[first, second] == 1 for first, second in model[\"states\"]\n        ], dtype=float),\n        \"mean_graph_distance\": np.array([\n            route_hop_distances[first, second] for first, second in model[\"states\"]\n        ], dtype=float),\n    }\n    pair_clustering_features[case] = features\n    reference = {name: float(feature @ model[\"pi\"]) for name, feature in features.items()}\n    assert np.isclose(reference[\"same_neighbourhood\"], 5 / 11)\n    assert np.isclose(reference[\"adjacent\"], 13 / 66)\n    assert np.isclose(reference[\"mean_graph_distance\"], 3)\n    if clustering_reference is None:\n        clustering_reference = reference\n    else:\n        assert all(np.isclose(reference[name], clustering_reference[name]) for name in reference)\n\n    curves = {name: [] for name in features}\n    vectors = joint_spectra[case][\"vectors\"]\n    eigenvalues = joint_spectra[case][\"eigenvalues\"]\n    start_coordinates = vectors[model[\"start_index\"], :]\n    for step in joint_curve_times:\n        # This spectral identity evaluates the exact finite-state law\n        # q_t = P**t e_start in floating point; it is not a sampled record.\n        probability = vectors @ (start_coordinates * eigenvalues**step)\n        for name, feature in features.items():\n            curves[name].append(float(feature @ probability))\n    joint_clustering_curves[case] = curves\n    # Reuse the separately computed matrix-power endpoint at t=100, so the\n    # printed summaries are independently checkable against the curve values.\n    joint_clustering_at_100[case] = {\n        name: float(feature @ joint_endpoint_100[case])\n        for name, feature in features.items()\n    }\n    time_100_index = joint_curve_times.index(100)\n    assert all(np.isclose(curves[name][time_100_index], value, atol=1e-11)\n               for name, value in joint_clustering_at_100[case].items())\n\nprint(\"Uniform 132-pair exclusion references:\")\nprint(\"Same neighbourhood: 5/11 =\", format(clustering_reference[\"same_neighbourhood\"], \".10f\"))\nprint(\"Adjacent stops: 13/66 =\", format(clustering_reference[\"adjacent\"], \".10f\"))\nprint(\"Mean graph distance: 3 hops\")\nfor case, values in joint_clustering_at_100.items():\n    print(case, \"t=100; same neighbourhood:\", format(values[\"same_neighbourhood\"], \".10f\"),\n          \"adjacent:\", format(values[\"adjacent\"], \".10f\"),\n          \"mean graph distance:\", format(values[\"mean_graph_distance\"], \".10f\"))\nprint(\"Labelled-pair proximity is not a truck count or evidence of attraction.\")\nprint(\"Graph distance counts edges, not travel time.\")\n\nclustering_title = \"Same neighbourhood?<br>Labelled pair · exact law\"\nclustering_traces = [\n    {\"x\": joint_curve_times, \"y\": curves[\"same_neighbourhood\"], \"name\": case,\n     \"mode\": \"lines+markers\", \"line\": {\"color\": plot_colours[case]}}\n    for case, curves in joint_clustering_curves.items()\n]\nclustering_traces.append({\n    \"x\": joint_curve_times,\n    \"y\": [clustering_reference[\"same_neighbourhood\"]] * len(joint_curve_times),\n    \"name\": \"5/11\", \"mode\": \"lines\", \"line\": {\"color\": plot_ink, \"dash\": \"dot\"},\n})\nmplot(clustering_traces, title=clustering_title, xlabel=\"Moves t · log scale\", ylabel=\"Pair probability\",\n      layout=spectral_layout(clustering_title,\n                             xaxis={\"type\": \"log\", \"automargin\": True},\n                             yaxis={\"range\": [0, 0.5], \"automargin\": True}))"
      },
      {
        "type": "markdown",
        "language": "markdown",
        "name": "occupation_bridge",
        "code": "## 6 · A running average measures the whole record\n\nNow sample a single seeded pair route. After each move, count its current pair—including waits and blocked proposals. The record contains $X_1,\\ldots,X_n$; the starting state $X_0$ is **not** counted. For pair $s$,\n\n$$\\widehat\\pi_n(s)=\\frac1n\\sum_{t=1}^{n}\\mathbf1\\{X_t=s\\}.$$\n\nEquivalently, update the running histogram recursively:\n\n$$\\widehat\\pi_n=\\widehat\\pi_{n-1}+\\frac{e_{X_n}-\\widehat\\pi_{n-1}}{n}.$$\n\nThe code stores integer counts and divides at checkpoints 100, 1,000 and 10,000; it computes the same estimator. The two heatmaps below use **10,000 visits**, with a common colour scale. They are not the endpoint laws above.\n\nEarly biased visits remain part of this average even after the endpoint law is close to stationary. Its deterministic expectation is\n\n$$\\mathbb E[\\widehat\\pi_n]=\\frac1n\\sum_{t=1}^{n}P_{\\rm pair}^{t}e_{(3,9)}.$$\n\nThe following distance plot separates the sampled histogram, this **expected histogram**, and the exact endpoint law. The expectation describes initialization bias, not a particular noisy route. Total variation is nonlinear: distance of the expected histogram is not the expected distance of a sampled histogram. A seeded route's error need not decrease at every checkpoint."
      },
      {
        "type": "code",
        "language": "python",
        "name": "sample_walks",
        "code": "\"\"\"Cell 4: seeded records, deterministic reference curves, and a variance check.\n\nAll randomness is local to explicit NumPy Generator instances.  No global\nrandom state, files, network, plots, or optional scientific packages are used.\n\"\"\"\n\n\ndef transition_cdf(transition):\n    \"\"\"CDF per SOURCE column, matching P[destination, source].\"\"\"\n    cumulative = np.cumsum(transition, axis=0)\n    # Guarantee a destination for every u < 1 despite summation roundoff.\n    cumulative[-1, :] = 1.0\n    return cumulative\n\n\ndef seeded_occupation_snapshots(model, checkpoints, seed):\n    \"\"\"Record X_1,...,X_n, including rejected/stay proposals as observations.\"\"\"\n    checkpoints = tuple(sorted({_positive_step_count(step) for step in checkpoints}))\n    if not checkpoints:\n        raise ValueError(\"At least one checkpoint is needed.\")\n    generator = np.random.default_rng(seed)\n    cumulative = transition_cdf(model[\"P\"])\n    current = int(model[\"start_index\"])\n    counts = np.zeros(model[\"P\"].shape[0], dtype=np.int64)\n    snapshots = {}\n    for step in range(1, checkpoints[-1] + 1):\n        current = int(np.searchsorted(cumulative[:, current], generator.random(), side=\"right\"))\n        counts[current] += 1\n        if step in checkpoints:\n            snapshots[step] = {\n                \"counts\": counts.copy(),\n                \"distribution\": counts.copy() / step,\n                \"endpoint_index\": current,\n                \"endpoint_pair\": model[\"states\"][current],\n            }\n    return snapshots\n\n\ndef stationary_variance_replications(model, eigensystem, step_count=2048, replications=128, seed=271828):\n    \"\"\"Independent stationary paths validate Var(mean psi)=tau_n/n.\n\n    This is a noisy Monte Carlo check with a modest replication count, not a\n    calibrated hypothesis test.  Starting uniformly is essential for comparing\n    with the stationary variance formula; the main paths start at (3,9).\n    \"\"\"\n    step_count = _positive_step_count(step_count)\n    replications = _positive_step_count(replications)\n    if replications < 2:\n        raise ValueError(\"At least two independent replications are required.\")\n    generator = np.random.default_rng(seed)\n    state_count = model[\"P\"].shape[0]\n    current = generator.integers(state_count, size=replications)\n    cumulative = transition_cdf(model[\"P\"])\n    counts = np.zeros((replications, state_count), dtype=np.int64)\n    replication_index = np.arange(replications)\n    for step in range(step_count):\n        uniforms = generator.random(replications)\n        # Each column selects the CDF of its current source state.  Counting\n        # thresholds below u is vectorized categorical sampling for all paths.\n        selected_cdfs = cumulative[:, current]\n        current = np.sum(selected_cdfs <= uniforms[None, :], axis=0)\n        # Each row identifies a distinct replication, so no repeated row/index\n        # pair occurs; this direct indexed += safely adds one count per path.\n        counts[replication_index, current] += 1\n    mode_means = (counts / step_count) @ eigensystem[\"psi\"][:, 1:]\n    tau = finite_n_tau(eigensystem[\"eigenvalues\"][1:], step_count)\n    theoretical_variance = tau / step_count\n    empirical_variance = mode_means.var(axis=0, ddof=1)\n    whitened_per_path = (step_count * mode_means**2 / tau).mean(axis=1)\n    return {\n        \"step_count\": step_count,\n        \"replications\": replications,\n        \"seed\": seed,\n        \"mode_means\": mode_means,\n        \"theoretical_variance\": theoretical_variance,\n        \"empirical_variance\": empirical_variance,\n        \"variance_ratio\": empirical_variance / theoretical_variance,\n        \"W\": whitened_per_path,\n        \"mean_W\": float(whitened_per_path.mean()),\n        \"standard_error_W\": float(whitened_per_path.std(ddof=1) / np.sqrt(replications)),\n        \"stationary_expected_W\": 1.0,\n    }\n\n\ndef summarize_occupation_case(model, eigensystem, snapshots):\n    \"\"\"Keep observed record, endpoint law, and expected record separate.\"\"\"\n    summaries = {}\n    for step_count, snapshot in snapshots.items():\n        observed_distribution = snapshot[\"distribution\"]\n        expected = exact_occupation_expectation(eigensystem, model[\"start_index\"], step_count)\n        endpoint = exact_endpoint_distribution(model[\"P\"], model[\"start_index\"], step_count)\n        whitened = spectral_whitening(observed_distribution, eigensystem, step_count)\n        uniform_stops = np.full(model[\"stop_count\"], 1.0 / model[\"stop_count\"])\n        empirical_pooled = pooled_stop_distribution(observed_distribution, model)\n        endpoint_pooled = pooled_stop_distribution(endpoint, model)\n        summaries[step_count] = {\n            \"empirical_tv\": total_variation(observed_distribution, model[\"pi\"]),\n            \"W\": whitened[\"W\"],\n            \"whitening\": whitened,\n            \"expected_occupation\": expected,\n            \"endpoint_distribution\": endpoint,\n            \"endpoint_tv\": total_variation(endpoint, model[\"pi\"]),\n            \"grid\": pair_distribution_grid(observed_distribution, model),\n            \"empirical_pooled\": empirical_pooled,\n            \"empirical_pooled_tv\": total_variation(empirical_pooled, uniform_stops),\n            \"endpoint_pooled\": endpoint_pooled,\n            \"endpoint_pooled_tv\": total_variation(endpoint_pooled, uniform_stops),\n        }\n    return summaries\n\n\njoint_visit_snapshots = {\n    name: seeded_occupation_snapshots(model, spectral_checkpoints, spectral_seed)\n    for name, model in joint_models.items()\n}\njoint_visit_summaries = {\n    name: summarize_occupation_case(model, joint_spectra[name], joint_visit_snapshots[name])\n    for name, model in joint_models.items()\n}\n# Independent stationary replications are a separate experiment, not more\n# samples appended to the one fixed-start record used in the heatmaps.\nstationary_variance_checks = {\n    name: stationary_variance_replications(model, joint_spectra[name], seed=spectral_seed + 1)\n    for name, model in joint_models.items()\n}"
      },
      {
        "type": "code",
        "language": "python",
        "name": "occupation_picture",
        "code": "visit_step = spectral_checkpoints[-1]\nvisit_colour_limit = max(float(joint_visit_snapshots[case][visit_step][\"distribution\"].max())\n                         for case in joint_models)\nfor case, model in joint_models.items():\n    distribution = joint_visit_snapshots[case][visit_step][\"distribution\"]\n    show_pair_map(distribution, model, case + \" bridge · visits<br>n = \" + str(visit_step) + \" · 12 × 12\",\n                  visit_colour_limit, \"Visit frequency\")\n    for step, summary in joint_visit_summaries[case].items():\n        print(case, step, \"visits; raw TV:\", round(summary[\"empirical_tv\"], 6),\n              \"stationary-reference W:\", round(summary[\"W\"], 6))\n\noccupation_traces = []\nfor case, summaries in joint_visit_summaries.items():\n    for kind, field, dash in ((\"sample\", \"empirical_tv\", \"solid\"),\n                              (\"mean record\", \"expected_occupation\", \"dash\"),\n                              (\"endpoint\", \"endpoint_tv\", \"dot\")):\n        values = [summary[field][\"bias_tv\"] if kind == \"mean record\" else summary[field]\n                  for summary in summaries.values()]\n        occupation_traces.append({\"x\": list(summaries), \"y\": values, \"name\": case + \" · \" + kind,\n                                  \"mode\": \"lines+markers\", \"line\": {\"color\": plot_colours[case], \"dash\": dash}})\noccupation_title = \"Three different distances<br>fixed start (3, 9)\"\noccupation_layout = spectral_layout(occupation_title, height=470,\n                                    xaxis={\"type\": \"log\", \"automargin\": True})\noccupation_layout[\"margin\"][\"b\"] = 140\nmplot(occupation_traces, title=occupation_title, xlabel=\"Recorded moves n · log scale\", ylabel=\"Total variation\",\n      layout=occupation_layout)"
      },
      {
        "type": "markdown",
        "language": "markdown",
        "name": "noise_bridge",
        "code": "## 7 · Expected noise depends on each mode's persistence\n\nProject the histogram error onto a nonconstant, unit-variance graph mode:\n\n$$a_k=\\sum_s(\\widehat\\pi_n(s)-\\pi_s)\\phi_k(s)\n     =\\frac1n\\sum_{t=1}^{n}\\phi_k(X_t).$$\n\nThe stationary term vanishes because $\\sum_s\\pi_s\\phi_k(s)=0$. **If the route starts in stationarity**, then $\\mathbb E[a_k]=0$ and\n\n$$\\operatorname{Var}(a_k)=\\frac{\\tau_k(n)}n,\\qquad\n\\tau_k(n)=1+2\\sum_{h=1}^{n-1}(1-h/n)\\rho_k^h.$$\n\nNearby observations along a persistent mode contain less independent information. For a long record,\n\n$$\\tau_k(\\infty)=\\frac{1+\\rho_k}{1-\\rho_k}\n =\\frac{2-\\mu_k}{\\mu_k}.$$\n\nFor a slow positive mode with $n\\mu_k\\gg1$, its standard error is approximately $\\sqrt{2/(n\\mu_k)}$—**not** $1/(\\sqrt n\\,\\mu_k)$. The first plot uses the exact finite-record formula, so it does not assume this long-record condition. These covariance ideas are developed in [Aldous and Fill's treatment of reversible walks](https://www.stat.berkeley.edu/~aldous/RWG/Book_Ralph/Ch4.S4.html).\n\nNormalize each mode's squared error by its stationary variance and average:\n\n$$W_n=\\frac{1}{M-1}\\sum_{k=1}^{M-1}\\frac{n a_k^2}{\\tau_k(n)}.$$\n\nFor a stationary start, $\\mathbb E[W_n]=1$ exactly; the pair model uses denominator **131**. This is a **noise-adjusted occupation score**, not proof that the endpoint mixed, not a calibrated confidence interval, and not an exact chi-square test. It downweights naturally noisy slow modes. Report raw histogram distance alongside it.\n\nOur main route starts at a fixed pair, not in stationarity. It can have nonzero mode means and different covariance, so **1 is only a stationary reference for its W**, not its exact expected value. In fact,\n\n$$\\mathbb E_{x}[a_k]=\\phi_k(x)\\frac{\\rho_k(1-\\rho_k^n)}{n(1-\\rho_k)}.$$\n\nThe leading $\\rho_k$ comes from counting after the first move. A burn-in can reduce this bias but does not make the stationary formulas automatically exact. Repeated eigenvalues admit different eigenvector bases; the plot groups equal-eigenvalue bands and averages their energies, preserving the interpretation across such basis changes."
      },
      {
        "type": "code",
        "language": "python",
        "name": "noise_picture",
        "code": "noise_lengths = [100, 300, 1000, 3000, 10000]\nnoise_traces = []\nfor case, spectrum in joint_spectra.items():\n    rho = spectrum[\"eigenvalues\"][1]\n    standard_errors = [float(np.sqrt(finite_n_tau(np.array([rho]), n)[0] / n)) for n in noise_lengths]\n    noise_traces.append({\"x\": noise_lengths, \"y\": standard_errors, \"name\": case,\n                         \"mode\": \"lines+markers\", \"line\": {\"color\": plot_colours[case]}})\nnoise_title = \"Slowest mode's uncertainty<br>stationary initialization\"\nmplot(noise_traces, title=noise_title, xlabel=\"Recorded moves n · log scale\", ylabel=\"Standard error\",\n      layout=spectral_layout(noise_title, xaxis={\"type\": \"log\", \"automargin\": True}))\n\nband_energy_traces = []\nfor case, spectrum in joint_spectra.items():\n    whitening = joint_visit_summaries[case][visit_step][\"whitening\"]\n    band_rates, band_energies = [], []\n    for band in joint_spectral_bands[case]:\n        # Band indices use full-spectrum numbering, excluding constant 0;\n        # the energy array starts at nonconstant mode 1, hence subtract 1.\n        indices = np.array(band[\"indices\"]) - 1\n        band_rates.append(1 - band[\"eigenvalue\"])\n        band_energies.append(float(whitening[\"mode_energy\"][indices].mean()))\n    band_energy_traces.append({\"x\": band_rates, \"y\": band_energies, \"name\": case,\n                               \"mode\": \"markers\", \"marker\": {\"color\": plot_colours[case], \"size\": 6}})\nenergy_title = \"Noise-adjusted band energy<br>one fixed-start record\"\nmplot(band_energy_traces, title=energy_title, xlabel=\"Decay rate μ · log scale\", ylabel=\"Energy per mode\",\n      layout=spectral_layout(energy_title, xaxis={\"type\": \"log\", \"automargin\": True}))\nprint(\"Band points average modes with equal eigenvalues; W weights every mode equally.\")\nprint(\"A fixed-start W near 1 does not certify endpoint mixing or histogram accuracy.\")"
      },
      {
        "type": "markdown",
        "language": "markdown",
        "name": "stationary_bridge",
        "code": "## 8 · Check the stationary reference in a separate experiment\n\nThe next picture uses **128 independent simulated routes of 2,048 moves each**, initialized uniformly over the 132 legal pairs. This is a different experiment from extending the one fixed-start route.\n\nFor each equal-eigenvalue band, compare empirical variance across these routes with $\\tau_k(n)/n$. Band averages avoid choosing a privileged basis within repeated modes. Ratios should fluctuate around 1; 128 replications do not produce an exact match. The printed mean W and its Monte Carlo standard error also check the reference $\\mathbb E[W]=1$.\n\nZero stationary covariance between different normalized modes does **not** establish independence. We use these replications as a numerical illustration, not a calibrated hypothesis test or real-world validation. The model's routing and exclusion assumptions remain the main limitations."
      },
      {
        "type": "code",
        "language": "python",
        "name": "stationary_check",
        "code": "variance_traces = []\nfor case, check in stationary_variance_checks.items():\n    band_rates, band_ratios = [], []\n    for band in joint_spectral_bands[case]:\n        indices = np.array(band[\"indices\"]) - 1\n        band_rates.append(1 - band[\"eigenvalue\"])\n        band_ratios.append(float(check[\"variance_ratio\"][indices].mean()))\n    variance_traces.append({\"x\": band_rates, \"y\": band_ratios, \"name\": case,\n                           \"mode\": \"markers\", \"marker\": {\"color\": plot_colours[case], \"size\": 6}})\n    print(case, \"stationary paths:\", check[\"replications\"], \"moves each:\", check[\"step_count\"])\n    print(\"Mean W:\", round(check[\"mean_W\"], 6), \"Monte Carlo SE:\", round(check[\"standard_error_W\"], 6),\n          \"theoretical expectation:\", check[\"stationary_expected_W\"])\nvariance_traces.append({\"x\": [0.002, 1], \"y\": [1, 1], \"name\": \"Reference 1\",\n                        \"line\": {\"color\": \"#e6edf3\", \"dash\": \"dot\"}})\nvariance_title = \"Variance formula check<br>128 stationary routes\"\nmplot(variance_traces, title=variance_title, xlabel=\"Decay rate μ · log scale\", ylabel=\"Empirical / theory\",\n      layout=spectral_layout(variance_title, xaxis={\"type\": \"log\", \"automargin\": True}))"
      },
      {
        "type": "markdown",
        "language": "markdown",
        "name": "periodic_bridge",
        "code": "## 9 · A periodic endpoint can have a balanced time average\n\nConsider two states. With stay probability $s$,\n\n$$P_s=\\begin{pmatrix}s&1-s\\\\1-s&s\\end{pmatrix},\\qquad\\rho_1=2s-1.$$\n\nFor $s=0$, the system alternates forever. Starting at state 0, the endpoint probability never converges to $(1/2,1/2)$, but visits collected over an even number of moves are exactly balanced. With $s=1/2$, the endpoint becomes uniform after one move. Its **expected** visit histogram is uniform too, but a particular finite sampled route need not have exactly balanced counts. The next plot averages endpoint laws to show this expected record; it does not simulate such a route.\n\nThis is **periodicity of a finite Markov chain**, not a nonlinear dynamical-system limit cycle. The delivery kernels contain waiting, so they avoid this forced alternation. The next short code cell handles the negative periodic eigenvalue directly; the earlier lazy-kernel helpers intentionally assumed eigenvalues in $[0,1]$."
      },
      {
        "type": "code",
        "language": "python",
        "name": "periodic_example",
        "code": "periodic_traces = []\nperiodic_results = {}\nfor stay_probability, colour in ((0.0, \"#fbbf24\"), (0.5, \"#63c5ff\")):\n    kernel = np.array([[stay_probability, 1 - stay_probability],\n                       [1 - stay_probability, stay_probability]])\n    endpoint = np.array([1.0, 0.0])\n    running_total = np.zeros(2)\n    endpoints, averages = [], []\n    for step in range(1, 13):\n        endpoint = kernel @ endpoint\n        running_total += endpoint\n        endpoints.append(float(endpoint[0]))\n        averages.append(float(running_total[0] / step))\n    periodic_results[stay_probability] = {\"endpoint\": endpoints, \"mean_record\": averages}\n    for kind, values, dash in ((\"endpoint\", endpoints, \"solid\"), (\"expected record\", averages, \"dot\")):\n        periodic_traces.append({\"x\": list(range(1, 13)), \"y\": values,\n                                \"name\": \"s=\" + str(stay_probability) + \" · \" + kind,\n                                \"mode\": \"lines+markers\", \"line\": {\"color\": colour, \"dash\": dash}})\nperiodic_title = \"Alternation versus waiting<br>probability at state 0\"\nmplot(periodic_traces, title=periodic_title, xlabel=\"Moves t\", ylabel=\"Probability\",\n      layout=spectral_layout(periodic_title, yaxis={\"range\": [-0.05, 1.05], \"automargin\": True}))"
      },
      {
        "type": "markdown",
        "language": "markdown",
        "name": "takeaways",
        "code": "## What to keep—and what to try next\n\n- An endpoint law and an occupation histogram are different distributions.\n- Endpoint entropy counts effective current-state possibilities; route-history entropy can keep growing after the endpoint distribution has mixed.\n- Two labelled trucks can look balanced after pooling while their joint law retains a slow label pattern.\n- Clustering requires a stated proximity measure and an exclusion-aware baseline. Our trucks wander randomly; they do not solve delivery routes or reroute around road congestion.\n- Forbidden states belong to the state graph, not to a missing-data interpolation problem. Relabelling heatmap axes cannot change the transition spectrum.\n- Noise in a correlated record depends on both sample length and mode persistence. W near 1 can coexist with substantial raw histogram error.\n- Waiting, connectivity, initialization and normalization are assumptions to check, not optional decoration.\n\n**Experiments:** change the seed; increase the checkpoints to 30,000; compare fixed-start bias with the endpoint curve; or give each stop two bays and rebuild the state rules. Do not simply fill the forbidden diagonal without changing the model. More realistic dispatch would require schedules, asymmetric traffic, service times and capacity information.\n\n**Related reading:** the existing **Markov Groups** workbook supplies the cube permutation example. [Aldous and Fill, spectral representation](https://www.stat.berkeley.edu/~aldous/RWG/Book_Ralph/Ch3.S4.html) develops reversible-chain modes and relaxation. [Glynn, simulation bias and variance](https://web.stanford.edu/~glynn/papers/1984/G84.html) discusses sample averages. [Shuman et al., graph signal processing](https://arxiv.org/abs/1211.0053) motivates graph-adapted rather than rectangular-image spectra. Spatial error spectra, spectral dimension and nonlinear limit cycles could each be a separate follow-up; this workbook does not claim to solve those topics."
      }
    ]
  }
}
